# Robust Search System Specification

**(Second Brain / Knowledge Capture Product)**

---

## 1. Product Goal

Build a **robust, low-friction search system** that allows users to retrieve saved content even when they:

* Remember only fragments
* Misspell words
* Recall ideas instead of exact text
* Forget when or where something was saved

### North-Star Outcome

> A tired user can find something they half-remember from weeks ago in under **5 seconds**, without thinking about how search works.

---

## 2. Core Principles

1. **High Recall > Perfect Precision**
2. **Fragments are First-Class Queries**
3. **Zero Organization Tax**
4. **Search feels helpful, not “smart”**
5. **Exact intent always beats semantic guesses**

---

## 3. Mental Model

Search is **not one engine**, but a **pipeline of cooperating engines**:

```
User Query
   ├─ Keyword Engine (exact / partial recall)
   ├─ Semantic Engine (meaning)
   └─ Context Engine (time, source, behavior)
```

Results are **merged and re-ranked**, not returned independently.

---

## 4. Data Ingestion & Indexing

### 4.1 Stored Fields (per item)

**Raw**

* Original content (HTML / text / OCR)
* URL or source identifier

**Cleaned**

* Boilerplate-removed text
* Normalized casing, punctuation

**Metadata**

* Source (web, reddit, screenshot, pdf, etc.)
* Timestamp (created, ingested)
* Device (mobile / desktop)

**Derived (mandatory)**

* 2–3 line summary
* Auto-topics (lightweight, non-taxonomic)
* Embedding vector
* Activity signals (open count, time spent)

> ⚠️ Never index only raw text.
> Always index **interpretations**.

---

## 5. Index Types (Minimum Viable)

| Index             | Purpose                  |
| ----------------- | ------------------------ |
| Full-text (BM25)  | Exact & partial keywords |
| Prefix index      | Partial word recall      |
| Edge n-gram index | Mid-word recall          |
| Vector index      | Semantic meaning         |
| Metadata index    | Time, source filters     |
| Activity index    | Personal relevance       |

---

## 6. Query Understanding (Intent Heuristics)

Lightweight classification **before searching**:

| Pattern                      | Interpreted As   |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------- |
| 1–2 words                    | Lookup           |
| Sentence / question          | Semantic         |
| “recent”, “last week”        | Temporal         |
| “pdf”, “tweet”, “screenshot” | Source filter    |
| Known project term           | Contextual boost |

Intent modifies **index weighting**, not results inclusion.

---

## 7. Partial Keyword Support (Critical)

Partial keyword ≠ fuzzy ≠ semantic
They fix **different recall failures**.

### 7.1 Prefix Search (Primary)

**Solves**

* `infla` → inflation
* `proj` → project

**Rules**

* Min length: 2 chars
* Applied to:

  * Titles
  * Headings
  * First 200 chars
  * Auto-extracted entities

**Ranking**

* Prefix > fuzzy > semantic
* Early-position matches boosted

---

### 7.2 Edge N-grams (Carefully Scoped)

**Solves**

* `gress` → progress
* `ation` → inflation

**Guardrails**

* Edge n-grams only (not full n-grams)
* Applied only to:

  * Titles
  * Headings
  * OCR text
* Min length: 3
* Max length: 10

⚠️ Do **not** apply to full body text.

---

### 7.3 Fuzzy Search (Last Resort)

**Solves**

* Typos
* Misspellings

**Rules**

* Edit distance ≤ 1 (≤2 only for long tokens)
* Disabled for queries < 4 chars
* Score penalty vs prefix/exact

---

### 7.4 Semantic Backstop

Used when:

* No strong prefix hits
* Query is fragmentary phrase

Rules:

* Semantic search reranks only
* Boost docs that contain partial token

---

## 8. Query Execution Order

```
If query length < 3:
  → Prefix search only
Else:
  → Prefix
  → Edge n-gram
  → Fuzzy
  → Semantic rerank
```

Results are merged → deduped → ranked.

---

## 9. Ranking Formula (Initial Weights)

```
Final Score =
  (0.35 × Semantic similarity)
+ (0.25 × Keyword relevance)
+ (0.20 × Recency proximity)
+ (0.10 × Source affinity)
+ (0.10 × Personal signals)
```

### Personal Signals

* Previously opened
* Time spent
* Highlighted / clipped

Cap contribution per signal to avoid dominance.

---

## 10. UX Behavior (Very Important)

### 10.1 Instant Feedback

* Results appear on first keystroke
* Ranking refines silently

### 10.2 Result Cards (Not Documents)

Each result shows:

* Matching snippet (highlighted)
* Why it matched
* Source + timestamp
* 1-line summary

### 10.3 Inline Interaction

* Open inline
* Expand preview
* Copy / send / link without navigation

---

## 11. Filters That Don’t Feel Like Filters

Instead of static checkboxes:

* Generate **contextual chips** from query

Examples:

* “Screenshots”
* “Last 7 days”
* “Reddit”
* “Project Alpha”

Tap to refine, never mandatory.

---

## 12. MVP Scope

### Phase 1 (Week 1–2)

* Full-text + prefix search
* Vector embeddings
* Hybrid ranking
* Recency boost
* Result cards

### Phase 2 (Week 3)

* OCR ingestion
* Query intent heuristics
* Source-aware boosting

### Phase 3 (Week 4)

* Inline preview
* “Why this result” explanation

---

## 13. Explicit Anti-Scope (Do NOT Build Yet)

❌ Manual tagging
❌ Folder hierarchies
❌ Boolean queries
❌ Saved searches
❌ Knowledge graphs

These add management overhead and break the “second brain” promise.

---

## 14. Quality Bar Tests

### Partial Recall Test

> Typing the **first 3 letters** of something saved should surface it in **top 3** results.

### Semantic Recall Test

> Describing the *idea* (not words) finds the right item in <5 seconds.

### Fatigue Test

> User should not need to rephrase query more than once.

---

## 15. Failure Modes to Watch

* Semantic overriding exact intent
* N-grams applied too broadly
* Fuzzy search dominating results
* Empty states during typing
* Over-exposed filters

---

## 16. Definition of “Robust”

Search is considered **robust** when:

* Users stop organizing manually
* Users trust fragments
* Users don’t think about search mechanics
* Users say:

  > “I don’t remember how I found it, but it worked.”

