# Search Evaluation Test Sets

**(Second Brain / Knowledge Capture System)**

---

## 1. Purpose of Evaluation

Search quality is **not binary** (“works / doesn’t work”).

We evaluate search on **human recall realism**:

* Fragmented memory
* Partial keywords
* Vague ideas
* Temporal uncertainty
* Fatigue and impatience

> The goal is to measure **trust**, not just relevance.

---

## 2. Ground Rules for All Tests

* All tests assume:

  * No manual tags
  * No folders
  * No user pre-filtering
* Results are considered **successful** if:

  * Correct item appears in **Top 3**
  * User does **not** need to reformulate more than once
* Measure:

  * Rank position
  * Time to first click
  * Query reformulations

---

## 3. Test Dataset Construction (Baseline)

Create a corpus of **~300 items** with:

### Content Mix

* 30% Web articles
* 20% Tweets / short posts
* 20% Screenshots (OCR-heavy)
* 15% PDFs
* 10% Notes / thoughts
* 5% Audio / video transcripts

### Time Distribution

* 40% last 7 days
* 30% last 30 days
* 20% last 90 days
* 10% >90 days

---

## 4. Test Set Categories

---

## A. Partial Keyword Recall Tests (Critical)

### A1. Prefix Recall

**User Memory**

> “I remember the word starting with `infl…`”

**Query**

```
infl
```

**Expected**

* Inflation-related item in Top 3
* Prefix match boosted over semantic-only items

**Failure Signals**

* Semantic results dominate
* Correct item appears below rank 5

---

### A2. Mid-Word Recall (Edge N-gram)

**User Memory**

> “Something ending with `gress`”

**Query**

```
gress
```

**Expected**

* “progress” / “congress” related items surface
* Title/headings favored over body matches

**Failure Signals**

* Random noise
* OCR text dominating incorrectly

---

### A3. Truncated Phrase

**User Memory**

> “It was something like `fed dot…`”

**Query**

```
fed dot
```

**Expected**

* Content mentioning “Fed dot plot”
* Partial phrase treated as exact intent

---

## B. Fuzzy Recall Tests

### B1. Simple Typo

**User Memory**

> “Inflation but I typed it wrong”

**Query**

```
infltion
```

**Expected**

* Inflation content in Top 3
* Fuzzy match penalized but not blocked

**Failure Signals**

* No relevant results
* Overly aggressive fuzzy matches

---

### B2. Phonetic Misspelling

**Query**

```
jerom powel
```

**Expected**

* Jerome Powell related content
* Name entity recognition helps recovery

---

## C. Semantic Recall Tests

### C1. Idea-Based Recall

**User Memory**

> “That thing about rates staying high longer”

**Query**

```
rates staying high longer
```

**Expected**

* Relevant macro / Fed content
* Semantic results boosted
* Keyword absence tolerated

---

### C2. Question-Style Query

**Query**

```
why did markets fall after cpi
```

**Expected**

* Analysis explaining CPI → market reaction
* Older but relevant content allowed

---

## D. Temporal Recall Tests

### D1. Vague Time Reference

**User Memory**

> “I saw it last week”

**Query**

```
last week inflation
```

**Expected**

* Temporal boost applied
* Last 7–10 day items ranked higher

---

### D2. Recency vs Relevance Conflict

**Query**

```
fed meeting summary
```

**Expected**

* Most recent Fed meeting summary
* Older summaries penalized unless heavily interacted with

---

## E. Source-Constrained Recall

### E1. Screenshot Recall

**User Memory**

> “It was a screenshot, not an article”

**Query**

```
screenshot inflation chart
```

**Expected**

* OCR-derived results
* Screenshot source boosted

---

### E2. Platform Recall

**Query**

```
reddit post about layoffs
```

**Expected**

* Reddit-sourced content boosted
* Cross-platform noise suppressed

---

## F. Personal Context Tests (Trust Builder)

### F1. Previously Opened Content

**User Memory**

> “I opened it a few times”

**Query**

```
inflation chart
```

**Expected**

* Previously opened item ranked above similar new ones

---

### F2. Highlighted / Interacted Content

**Query**

```
important risk
```

**Expected**

* Highlighted notes boosted
* Passive content ranked lower

---

## G. Fatigue & UX Tests

### G1. One-Word Query Under Fatigue

**Query**

```
fed
```

**Expected**

* High-confidence, diverse results
* No empty state
* No need to type more

---

### G2. Progressive Typing Stability

**Typing Sequence**

```
i → in → infl → infla
```

**Expected**

* Results refine smoothly
* No sudden disappearance of correct item

---

## 5. Scoring & Metrics

### Per-Test Metrics

* Rank of correct item
* Time to first click
* Query reformulations
* Scroll depth

### Aggregate Metrics

* % tests with correct result in Top 3
* Avg reformulations per task
* Avg time to success

---

## 6. Pass / Fail Criteria

### MVP Bar

* ≥70% tests pass (Top 3)
* Avg reformulations ≤1.5

### Strong Search

* ≥85% tests pass
* Avg reformulations ≤1
* Median time to success <5s

### Category-Defining

* ≥90% tests pass
* Users stop using manual organization
* Qualitative feedback:

  > “I don’t remember how, but it finds it”

---

## 7. Regression Policy

* Run full test suite:

  * Before any ranking change
  * After index schema change
  * After embedding model updates
* Lock partial-keyword tests as **non-regressible**

---

## 8. Final Truth Test

Ask users post-search:

> “Did you find what you were thinking of, or did you settle?”

If they *settled*, search failed — even if metrics passed.

